TOTP_ERR_UNSUPPORTED=This server does not offer two-step sign-in.
TOTP_ERR_PASSWORD=The password is not right.
TOTP_ERR_CODE=That code is not right.
TOTP_ERR_WAIT=Too many wrong codes. Wait {seconds} s and try again.
TOTP_ERR_ALREADY=It is already on. Turn it off first.
TOTP_ERR_FORBIDDEN=This account may not change its sign-in.
TOTP_ERR_HTTP=The server refused the request (HTTP {status}).
TOTP_ERR_NETWORK=Could not reach the server.
TOTP_RESET_BUTTON=Remove two-step sign-in
TOTP_RESET_CONFIRM=Remove the two-step sign-in of {username}? They can turn it on again afterwards.
TOTP_RESET_DONE=Two-step sign-in removed for {username}.
TOTP_RESET_ERROR=Could not remove the two-step sign-in.
MSG_NET_LOGIN_NEEDS_CODE=This account asks for a second step. Enter your code.
LBL_LOGIN_CODE=Two-step code or recovery code
ACCESS_WHAT_DISABLE=Disabling the robot
ACCESS_WHAT_ENABLE=Enabling the robot
ACCESS_DISABLE_HINT=Puts the robot in safe mode: it stops, its valves and pumps go off and the server refuses its commands except STOP.
ACCESS_ENABLE_HINT=Brings the robot out of safe mode. Nothing restarts and the jog has to be armed again.
ACCESS_CONFIRM_ENABLE=Enable this robot? It stays stopped and accepts commands again.
ACCESS_SAFE_MODE_BADGE=Safe mode - disabled
MSG_ACCESS_ROBOT_DISABLED={what} refused: the robot is in safe mode (disabled). Only STOP works on it until an administrator enables it again. {detail}
MSG_ACCESS_SERVER_READ_ONLY={what} refused: the server is in read-only mode. Only STOP and the safe-mode disable go through. {detail}
MSG_ACCESS_ENABLE_NOT_ALLOWED={what} refused: the server does not allow enabling a robot from a client (the option is off). An administrator can turn it on in Server Admin. {detail}
MSG_ACCESS_ADMIN_REQUIRED={what} refused: only an administrator can enable a robot. {detail}
MSG_NET_REJ_DISABLED={what} rejected: the robot is in safe mode (disabled); only STOP works on it until an administrator enables it ({detail})
MSG_NET_REJ_READ_ONLY={what} rejected: the server is in read-only mode; only STOP and the safe-mode disable go through ({detail})
LBL_SECURITY_ROBOTS=Safe mode of the robots
TITLE_SECURITY_ROBOTS=Safe mode of the robots
LBL_SECURITY_ROBOTS_ENABLE=Allow a client to enable a robot that is in safe mode (administrators only; off by default)
LBL_SECURITY_ROBOTS_HELP=Disabling a robot is always possible, like a stop. Without this option a disabled robot can only be brought back from the settings of an administrator.
MSG_SECURITY_ROBOTS_CONFIRM=Allow clients to enable robots that are in safe mode? An administrator will be able to bring a robot back with one click.
SECURITY_ROBOTS_WARNING=Administrators can now enable robots from a client.
LBL_SECURITY_HEALTH=Health route
TITLE_SECURITY_HEALTH=Health route
LBL_SECURITY_HEALTH_OPEN=Let anyone on the network read GET /health without signing in
LBL_SECURITY_HEALTH_HELP=Off by default: the route then answers as if it did not exist. When on, it shows only a bare health report, for a supervisor or load balancer.
MSG_SECURITY_HEALTH_CONFIRM=GET /health will answer without a sign-in. Open it?
SECURITY_HEALTH_WARNING=GET /health now answers without a sign-in (only a bare health report).
LBL_SECURITY_AUTH=Sign-in tokens
TITLE_SECURITY_AUTH=Sign-in tokens
LBL_SECURITY_AUTH_HS256=HS256 (shared secret, default)
LBL_SECURITY_AUTH_EDDSA=EdDSA (Ed25519, public keys published for other services)
LBL_SECURITY_AUTH_ACCEPT_HS256=Accept HS256 tokens while new tokens are signed with EdDSA
LBL_SECURITY_AUTH_HELP=HS256 by default (shared secret). With EdDSA the server publishes its public keys at /.well-known/jwks.json so another service can verify a token without a secret that could forge one. Keep HS256 accepted until the sessions of before the change have ended.
MSG_SECURITY_AUTH_CONFIRM=HS256 tokens will stop being accepted and the sessions that hold one must sign in again. Continue?
BTN_SECURITY_AUTH_ROTATE=Rotate the signing key
MSG_SECURITY_AUTH_ROTATE_CONFIRM=A new key will sign from now on; the previous one stays valid during the overlap. Rotate?
SECURITY_AUTH_EDDSA_WARNING=New tokens are now signed with EdDSA; the public keys are published at /.well-known/jwks.json.
SECURITY_AUTH_HS256_REFUSED_WARNING=HS256 tokens are no longer accepted: sessions opened before the change must sign in again.
SECURITY_AUTH_ROTATED=The signing key was rotated.
SECURITY_AUTH_ROTATE_ERROR=The key could not be rotated.
LBL_SECURITY_STOP=Global stop announcement
LBL_SECURITY_STOP_PUBLISH=Announce a global stop on the MQTT bridge (retained SAFE_STOP)
LBL_SECURITY_STOP_HELP=Off by default. Needs the MQTT bridge on. The state stays until an administrator releases it.
TITLE_SECURITY_JD=Job dispatcher
LBL_SECURITY_JD_RELAY=Deliver the stop requests of the job dispatcher to the robots (off by default)
LBL_SECURITY_JD_RELAY_HELP=The server reads the stop requests, stops the robot named there and only then acknowledges them. The switch applies on the next start of the server; the robot map applies at once.
LBL_SECURITY_JD_MAP=Robot map (one per line: dispatcher robot id = robot number in this server)
LBL_SECURITY_JD_MAP_PLACEHOLDER=arm-1 = 1
LBL_SECURITY_JD_MAP_HELP=A robot that is not in the map is never stopped nor acknowledged.
MSG_SECURITY_JD_MAP_INVALID=The robot map is not valid: write one line per robot as "id = number" (an id of 1 to 64 letters, digits, . _ : - and a number of 0 or more).
LBL_SECURITY_JD_REPLAN=Replanning of the job dispatcher
LBL_SECURITY_JD_ALLOW=Send a job back to the queue even if the silent robot never confirmed its stop (off by default)
LBL_SECURITY_JD_TIMEOUT=Seconds to wait for the confirmed stop (more than 0, at most 86400)
MSG_SECURITY_JD_TIMEOUT_INVALID=The wait must be a number greater than 0 and at most 86400.
LBL_SECURITY_JD_REPLAN_HELP=The server writes these two values into the configuration file of the dispatcher and asks it to read the file again; if it cannot, the dispatcher reads them when it starts.
LBL_SECURITY_JD_NOT_EDITABLE=The deployment did not name the configuration file of the dispatcher (HYDRA_UMC_JOB_DISPATCHER_CONFIG): these two options cannot be changed here.
MSG_SECURITY_JD_CONFIRM=Switch the delivery of stop requests on, or let a job go to another robot without a confirmed stop? Check the robot map first.
SECURITY_JD_WARNING_RELAY=The delivery of the stop requests is on; it starts working at the next start of the server.
SECURITY_JD_WARNING_REPLAN=A job can now go to another robot even if the silent robot never confirmed its stop.
LBL_SECURITY_JD_EXEC_TITLE=Running the assigned jobs
LBL_SECURITY_JD_EXEC_ENABLED=Run the jobs the job dispatcher assigns to the robots of this server (off by default)
LBL_SECURITY_JD_EXEC_ENABLED_HELP=When on it only decides and writes in the log what it would start (a dry run). It applies on the next start of the server.
LBL_SECURITY_JD_EXEC_LIVE=Really move the robots (leave the dry run)
LBL_SECURITY_JD_EXEC_LIVE_HELP=Off by default. When on, the server plays the recorded trajectory of the robot of an assigned job and reports the job as done when it ends. It never starts while the server is stopped or read-only. It applies on the next start of the server.
MSG_SECURITY_JD_EXEC_CONFIRM=Switching this on makes the robots move by themselves when the dispatcher assigns them a job. Check the robot map and the recorded trajectories first. Continue?
SECURITY_JD_WARNING_EXEC=The executor of the assigned jobs is on as a dry run; it starts at the next start of the server.
SECURITY_JD_WARNING_EXEC_LIVE=The executor will move the robots by itself after the next start of the server.
LBL_SECURITY_PRODUCTION=Production flash
LBL_SECURITY_PRODUCTION_RDP=Set the read-out protection (level 1) when a controller board is flashed in production
LBL_SECURITY_PRODUCTION_HELP=Off by default (level 0: nothing is protected). It only records the choice: no flashing tool reads it yet. Level 2 is permanent and is never offered.
TITLE_SECURITY_PRODUCTION=Production flash
MSG_SECURITY_PRODUCTION_CONFIRM=Turn on read-out protection for production flashes? Level 1 locks the reading of the chip through the debug port; only a full erase of the chip undoes it.
LBL_SECURITY_INGEST=What the server accepts from the services
LBL_SECURITY_INGEST_HELP=The server can keep the faults of the machines, the outcomes of reproduction scenarios, the status of the developer node, the verdicts of the anomaly detector and a signed record of the voice commands, and can check signed trajectory proposals. Everything is off by default; turning one on asks for a confirmation and every change is recorded. Only an administrator can change it.
LBL_SECURITY_INGEST_FAULTFEED=Faults of the machines (read from the broker)
LBL_SECURITY_INGEST_FAULTFEED_HINT=The server reads the fault messages of the broker, read only, and offers them to the clients.
LBL_SECURITY_INGEST_SCENARIO=Outcomes of reproduction scenarios
LBL_SECURITY_INGEST_SCENARIO_HINT=An operator or a service can send the outcome of a run; the server keeps it in a signed, chained record.
LBL_SECURITY_INGEST_DEVELOPERNODE=Status of the developer node
LBL_SECURITY_INGEST_DEVELOPERNODE_HINT=The developer node sends its status with the token of that node; the server keeps the latest one of each node.
LBL_SECURITY_INGEST_ANOMALYFEED=Verdicts of the anomaly detector (read from the broker)
LBL_SECURITY_INGEST_ANOMALYFEED_HINT=The server reads the verdicts, read only, and offers the latest one of each series.
LBL_SECURITY_INGEST_ANOMALYVOICERELAY=Tell the voice interface when a verdict turns suspect or anomalous
LBL_SECURITY_INGEST_ANOMALYVOICERELAY_HINT=Needs the verdicts above and the voice interface configured on the server.
LBL_SECURITY_INGEST_VOICECHAIN=Signed record of the voice commands
LBL_SECURITY_INGEST_VOICECHAIN_HINT=Every voice turn and confirmation becomes an entry signed by the server and chained to the one before. It holds codes and a hash of the person, never the text.
LBL_SECURITY_INGEST_PROPOSALVERIFY=Check of signed trajectory proposals
LBL_SECURITY_INGEST_PROPOSALVERIFY_HINT=Lets an operator ask whether a proposal signed with Ed25519 is genuine, current and for that robot. It never moves a robot.
LBL_SECURITY_INGEST_LOCKED=Set in the environment of the server: remove the variable there to change it here.
LBL_SECURITY_INGEST_KEYS=File with the public keys of the operators
LBL_SECURITY_INGEST_KEYS_HINT=A file name inside the data folder of the server, with the public keys that sign proposals. Leave it empty for none.
LBL_SECURITY_INGEST_KEYS_INVALID=Use a plain file name: letters, digits, dot, dash and underscore.
LBL_SECURITY_INGEST_RESTART=The feeds that read the broker apply on the next start of the server.
TITLE_SECURITY_INGEST=Turn on what the server accepts?
MSG_SECURITY_INGEST_CONFIRM=The server will accept and keep what the chosen services hand to it, or trust other public keys for proposals. Turn on only what you need; every change is recorded.
DEVNODE_TITLE=Developer node status
DEVNODE_DESC=The latest status that each developer node sent to the server.
DEVNODE_REFRESH=Refresh
DEVNODE_VERIFY=Check the signed record
DEVNODE_CHAIN_OK=The signed record is intact.
DEVNODE_CHAIN_BROKEN=The signed record is broken: do not trust these entries.
DEVNODE_NONE=No developer node has sent its status yet.
DEVNODE_OFF=Not available: turn on the status of the developer node above, or this server does not offer it.
DEVNODE_LOAD_ERROR=The status could not be read.
DEVNODE_FORBIDDEN=The status could not be read.
DEVNODE_RECEIVED=Received
DEVNODE_ENTRY=Entry
LBL_SECURITY_CONNECTIONS=Connection history
LBL_SECURITY_CONNECTIONS_RECORD=Keep the address and user agent of each client in the connection history
LBL_SECURITY_CONNECTIONS_HELP=Off by default: the history works without them. When on, only administrators are shown them.
TITLE_SECURITY_CONNECTIONS=Connection history
MSG_SECURITY_CONNECTIONS_CONFIRM=The address and user agent are personal data. Keep them in the connection history?
SECURITY_CONNECTIONS_WARNING=The server now keeps the address and user agent of every client in the connection history (only administrators see them).
LBL_SECURITY_RL=Request limits per address
LBL_SECURITY_RL_ENABLED=Limit the requests per address (sign-in, renewals, API, code and service routes, global stop)
LBL_SECURITY_RL_HELP=Each number is the most requests one address may make inside its window; the sign-in counts failed attempts only. A number fixed by the environment of the server is locked. A change applies at once and the counters start again.
LBL_SECURITY_RL_LOGIN_MAX=Failed sign-ins allowed
LBL_SECURITY_RL_LOGIN_WINDOW=Sign-in window (seconds)
LBL_SECURITY_RL_REFRESH_MAX=Token renewals allowed
LBL_SECURITY_RL_API_MAX=API requests allowed
LBL_SECURITY_RL_SENSITIVE_MAX=Requests allowed on code and service routes
LBL_SECURITY_RL_API_WINDOW=API window (seconds)
LBL_SECURITY_RL_STOPALL_MAX=Global stop requests allowed
LBL_SECURITY_RL_STOPALL_WINDOW=Global stop window (seconds)
BTN_SECURITY_RL_SAVE=Save the limits
TITLE_SECURITY_RL=Request limits per address
MSG_SECURITY_RL_CONFIRM=This lowers the protection of the server (an address could make any number of requests). Continue?
SECURITY_RL_OFF_WARNING=The request limits are OFF: nothing stops a loop of requests or a password guesser coming from one address.
SECURITY_RL_LOOSE_WARNING=A limit is set very high: it hardly protects the server any more.
SECURITY_RL_INVALID=Every number must be a whole number inside its range.
LBL_SECURITY_EXPOSURE=Exposure and logs
LBL_SECURITY_EXPOSURE_CAMERA=Let the camera pictures open without a ticket or a sign-in
LBL_SECURITY_EXPOSURE_WS=Accept a token in the WebSocket address (/ws?token=)
LBL_SECURITY_EXPOSURE_LOG=Blank out secrets in the log
LBL_SECURITY_EXPOSURE_VOICE=Require a verified signature on a confirmed spoken request
LBL_SECURITY_EXPOSURE_HELP=Safe defaults: pictures ask for a ticket, no token in the address, secrets blanked out, signature not required (turn it on once the voice service shares its key). A value fixed by the server environment cannot be changed here.
TITLE_SECURITY_EXPOSURE=Exposure and logs
MSG_SECURITY_EXPOSURE_CONFIRM=This lowers the protection of the server (open camera pictures, a token in the address, secrets in the log or no signature on spoken confirmations). Continue?
SECURITY_EXPOSURE_CAMERA_WARNING=The camera pictures are now open to the whole network.
SECURITY_EXPOSURE_WS_WARNING=A token in the WebSocket address is now accepted; proxies and servers may record it.
SECURITY_EXPOSURE_LOG_WARNING=Secrets are no longer blanked out of the log.
LBL_SECURITY_INSTALL=Installation and camera passwords
LBL_SECURITY_INSTALL_CAMERA=Hand the password of an IP camera to its process through the environment (not on its command line)
LBL_SECURITY_INSTALL_TLS=Let a new installation switch TLS on by itself
LBL_SECURITY_INSTALL_HELP=Safe defaults: both on. The camera option applies the next time a camera process starts; the TLS option is only read at the first start of an installation. A value fixed by the server environment cannot be changed here.
TITLE_SECURITY_INSTALL=Installation and camera passwords
MSG_SECURITY_INSTALL_CONFIRM=This lowers the protection of the server (a camera password on a command line, or no TLS by default in a new installation). Continue?
SECURITY_INSTALL_CAMERA_WARNING=The password of an IP camera is now on the command line of its process, where other users of the machine can read it.
SECURITY_INSTALL_TLS_WARNING=A new installation will no longer switch TLS on by itself.
