TOOLARMS_TITLE=Tool arm state
TOOLARMS_REFRESH=Refresh
TOOLARMS_DESC=Whether the power stage of each URTC tool may act, as the safety zones service last heard it. Read only: nothing here arms, disarms or stops a tool.
TOOLARMS_UNAVAILABLE=The tool arm state is not available: the safety zones service is not connected to the server or was not started with its tool list.
TOOLARMS_FORBIDDEN=This account may not read the tool arm state.
TOOLARMS_LOAD_ERROR=The tool arm state could not be read.
TOOLARMS_SUMMARY={armed} armed, {stale} with an old report (a report older than {ttl} s is stale)
TOOLARMS_EMPTY=No tool has reported yet.
TOOLARMS_TOOL=Tool {id}
TOOLARMS_LEVEL_ARMED=Armed
TOOLARMS_LEVEL_WARNING=Armed, report is old
TOOLARMS_LEVEL_UNKNOWN=Unknown, report is old
TOOLARMS_LEVEL_DISARMED=Disarmed
TOOLARMS_AGE=reported {value} {unit} ago
TOOLARMS_UNIT_S=s
TOOLARMS_UNIT_MIN=min
TOOLARMS_UNIT_H=h
TOOLARMS_BY=by {by}
TOOLARMS_STALE_NOTE=An old report is never shown as safe: an armed tool with an old report is a warning and a disarmed one is unknown.
DETHEF_TITLE=Reports of loaded models (DETECTION-HEF)
DETHEF_DESC=Lets the detection service accept the reports of the models it has loaded. Off by default. The server writes the settings file that the service re-reads while it runs.
DETHEF_LABEL=Accept the reports of loaded models
DETHEF_HINT=While it is off, the service refuses those reports and forgets the ones it had.
DETHEF_LOCKED=This value is fixed by the configuration of the server and cannot be changed here.
DETHEF_SAVED=Saved. The service picks the change up within a few seconds.
DETHEF_SAVE_ERROR=The change was refused: {reason}
DETHEF_FORBIDDEN=Only an administrator can change this setting.
DETHEF_LOAD_ERROR=The setting could not be read.
DETHEF_CONFIRM_TITLE=Accept the reports of loaded models?
DETHEF_CONFIRM_MESSAGE=The detection service will accept the reports that tell which models are loaded. Turn this on only if you trust the machines that send them.
DETHEF_CONFIRM_BUTTON=Accept
DOCK_SETUP_HELP=Help by level
HELP_TITLE=Help by level
HELP_INTRO=Step-by-step guides for the Setup screens and the administration of the server. Choose a level: basic for everyday tasks, advanced for the changes that need an administrator, technical for what happens behind the screen. The level you choose is kept for the next time.
HELP_SEARCH=Search the guides
HELP_LEVEL_ALL=All levels
HELP_LEVEL_BASIC=Basic
HELP_LEVEL_ADVANCED=Advanced
HELP_LEVEL_TECHNICAL=Technical
HELP_NO_MATCH=No guide matches. Try fewer words or another level.
HELP_VERSIONS_Q=Where do I see which versions are installed?
HELP_VERSIONS_S1=Open {eco} > {versions}.
HELP_VERSIONS_S2=The first line is this program; below it come the server and each program the server reports.
HELP_VERSIONS_S3=Press Refresh to read them again. A version that could not be read says it is not known.
HELP_CHECKLIST_Q=How do I know what is left to do on a new installation?
HELP_CHECKLIST_S1=Open {eco} > {installation}.
HELP_CHECKLIST_S2=Each step is marked done, to do or not known; the line above the list names the next step.
HELP_CHECKLIST_S3=Do the next step where it says, then press Refresh to see it ticked.
HELP_REMOTE_Q=How do I let a phone or a tablet reach the server?
HELP_REMOTE_S1=Open {eco} > {remote}: it shows the address the apps type, with its QR code.
HELP_REMOTE_S2=Turn on only the kinds of app that should find the server.
HELP_REMOTE_S3=To shut it all at once press Close all; it asks before it does.
HELP_HISTORY_Q=Where do I see who connected and what changed?
HELP_HISTORY_S1=Open {eco} > {history}.
HELP_HISTORY_S2=Connections lists each app and account with its sessions; the audit record lists the changes that matter.
HELP_HISTORY_S3=The audit record is shown to administrators only.
HELP_SECURITY_Q=How do I change the key that signs the sign-in tokens?
HELP_SECURITY_S1=Open {eco} > {security} as an administrator.
HELP_SECURITY_S2=Turn on the switch that allows a rotation; it is off again every time the screen opens.
HELP_SECURITY_S3=Press the rotation button and confirm. The old key stays accepted for the overlap time the server sets.
HELP_PUSH_Q=How do I see the state of the push notifications?
HELP_PUSH_S1=Open {eco} > {notifications} as an administrator.
HELP_PUSH_S2=It says whether the server is ready to send them, how many devices are registered and which events are on.
HELP_PUSH_S3=The screen only reads; nothing is changed from it.
HELP_TOOLARMS_Q=How do I see which tools are armed?
HELP_TOOLARMS_S1=Open {eco} > {admin}; the arm state of the tools is a panel of that screen.
HELP_TOOLARMS_S2=Each row says whether the power stage of a tool may act, who reported it and how old the report is. Press Refresh to read it again.
HELP_TOOLARMS_S3=A tool whose report is old is never shown as safe: armed with an old report is a warning, disarmed is unknown. Nothing here arms or stops a tool.
HELP_IDENTITY_Q=How do I make the server present its identity to the services?
HELP_IDENTITY_S1=Open {eco} > {admin} as an administrator and find Identity of the services.
HELP_IDENTITY_S2=Turn the switch on and confirm. It is off by default.
HELP_IDENTITY_S3=Giving each service its key is done on the machine, as root; this screen only changes the switch of the server.
HELP_DETECTIONHEF_Q=How do I let the detection service accept the reports of loaded models?
HELP_DETECTIONHEF_S1=Open {eco} > {admin} as an administrator and find Reports of loaded models.
HELP_DETECTIONHEF_S2=Turn the switch on and confirm. It is off by default; if the environment of the server fixes it, it is locked.
HELP_DETECTIONHEF_S3=The server writes a settings file that the service re-reads within seconds; no restart is needed.
HELP_UNKNOWN_Q=What does "not known" mean on the Setup screens?
HELP_UNKNOWN_S1=Something that could not be read is shown as not known, never as to do.
HELP_UNKNOWN_S2=The cause is shown at the top of the screen: no connection, an account that may not read it, or a server that predates the route.
HELP_UNKNOWN_S3=Press Refresh after fixing the cause.
DOCK_SETUP_PREFERENCES=Preferences
PREFS_TITLE=Preferences
PREFS_INTRO=Personal preferences of this program. They are kept in its own configuration file, never sent to a server, and applied at once.
PREFS_SCALE_TITLE=Size of the interface
PREFS_SCALE_HINT=Multiplies every text of the interface. 100 % is the normal size.
PREFS_LEVEL_TITLE=Level the Help opens at
PREFS_LEVEL_HINT=The Help by level opens at this level; it can be changed there as well.
PREFS_NOTE=Reduced animations are not offered here: this interface has no animation to reduce.
LIVEACT_TITLE=Live Activity on the lock screen
LIVEACT_DESC=The card of a robot that an iPhone shows on its lock screen while a job runs, updated by the Apple push service. Off by default.
LIVEACT_LABEL=Show the Live Activity of the robots on the lock screen
LIVEACT_PUSH_OFF=Push notifications are off, so the card is not sent even if this is on. Turn them on in the push settings.
LIVEACT_LEVEL_TITLE=What the card shows
LIVEACT_LEVEL_STATUS=Only the state
LIVEACT_LEVEL_STATUS_HINT=Running, idle, stopped, error or finished. Nothing else. This is the default.
LIVEACT_LEVEL_PROGRESS=The state and the progress
LIVEACT_LEVEL_PROGRESS_HINT=The state, the percentage and the step of the job.
LIVEACT_LEVEL_FULL=The whole panel
LIVEACT_LEVEL_FULL_HINT=The state, the progress and the panel of the robot: its name, model, tool and the position of its joints.
LIVEACT_LOCK_SCREEN_NOTE=A lock screen can be read by anyone who holds the phone. The server applies this level before anything leaves it: a phone cannot ask for more.
LIVEACT_LOCKED=This value is fixed by the configuration of the server and cannot be changed here.
LIVEACT_SAVED=Saved. The cards that follow use the new level.
LIVEACT_SAVE_ERROR=The change was refused: {reason}
LIVEACT_FORBIDDEN=Only an administrator can change this setting.
LIVEACT_LOAD_ERROR=The setting could not be read.
LIVEACT_CONFIRM_TITLE=Show the robots on the lock screen?
LIVEACT_CONFIRM_MESSAGE_STATUS=Anyone who holds an iPhone with a Live Activity will see the state of the robot on its lock screen.
LIVEACT_CONFIRM_MESSAGE_PROGRESS=Anyone who holds an iPhone with a Live Activity will see the state and the progress of the job on its lock screen.
LIVEACT_CONFIRM_MESSAGE_FULL=Anyone who holds an iPhone with a Live Activity will see the whole panel of the robot on its lock screen: its name, model, tool and the position of its joints.
LIVEACT_CONFIRM_BUTTON=Show it

# The live state of the twin through the server (Server Admin)
TWINLIVE_TITLE=Live state of the twin
TWINLIVE_DESC=Watch the state the digital twin pushes (the joints of the robot and the vehicles it follows) through this server. It is off by default and only a choice of this browser; this window only reads and nothing it sends reaches the twin.
TWINLIVE_LABEL=Watch the live state of the twin
TWINLIVE_OFF=Not watching.
TWINLIVE_CONNECTING=Connecting to the twin through the server...
TWINLIVE_LIVE=Connected: the server passes on what the twin pushes.
TWINLIVE_DENIED=The session was refused: sign in again to watch the twin.
TWINLIVE_NOT_AVAILABLE=The server cannot pass on the twin now: it is not set up, or too many panels are watching.
TWINLIVE_UNAVAILABLE=The twin cannot be reached; trying again shortly.
TWINLIVE_CLOSED=The connection closed; trying again shortly.
TWINLIVE_STATE_REAL=State of the real robot: {joints} joints.
TWINLIVE_STATE_SIMULATED=State of a simulation: {joints} joints.
TWINLIVE_NO_STATE=No state received yet.
TWINLIVE_STATUS=The twin reports: {code}
TWINLIVE_VEHICLES=Vehicles: {count} ({stale} with an old report)

# The checks of an image before a flash over the real bus
FLASHER_PREFLIGHT_NO_HARDWARE_ID=This image has no hardware id (a local file): pick it from the release so the board can check it is for it.
FLASHER_PREFLIGHT_NO_VERSION=This image has no version: pick it from the release so the board can check it against the installed one.
FLASHER_PREFLIGHT_DOWNGRADE_BLOCKED=This image is older than the installed version: tick the downgrade choice if that is what you want.
FLASHER_PREFLIGHT_FRAM_ERASE_UNSUPPORTED=Erasing the F-RAM is not offered: the server or the bridge has it switched off (or the version was not read). Untick it.
STOP_STATE_STOPPED=STOPPED
STOP_STATE_READY=Ready
STOP_STATE_UNKNOWN=Stop not monitored
STOP_STATE_TOOLTIP=Physical emergency stop: {physical} - global stop: {software}
STOP_STATE_PHYSICAL_LATCHED=latched
STOP_STATE_PHYSICAL_READY=released
STOP_STATE_PHYSICAL_UNKNOWN=not monitored
STOP_STATE_SOFTWARE_STOPPED=on
STOP_STATE_SOFTWARE_READY=off
TITLE_FRAMOP=F-RAM of the kinematic brain
LBL_FRAMOP_UNKNOWN=Read the version first: the server and the bridge say here whether the F-RAM operations are offered.
LBL_FRAMOP_UNSUPPORTED=The F-RAM operations are not offered. They are off by default: turn them on in Server Admin > Security (F-RAM erase) and start the bridge with --enable-fram-erase.
BTN_FRAMOP_SELFTEST=Test F-RAM
BTN_FRAMOP_ERASE=Erase F-RAM...
TITLE_FRAMOP_ERASE=Erase the whole F-RAM
MSG_FRAMOP_ERASE_BODY=This writes zeros over all 8192 bytes of the F-RAM of the kinematic brain, with the stack running, and cannot be undone. Type {word} to confirm.
LBL_FRAMOP_ERASE_PLACEHOLDER=Type {word}
BTN_FRAMOP_ERASE_CONFIRM=Erase
LBL_FRAMOP_BUSY=Working on the F-RAM...
MSG_FRAMOP_RESULT_ERASE_OK=F-RAM erased: {value} bytes written and read back.
MSG_FRAMOP_RESULT_SELFTEST_OK=F-RAM self-test passed; the contents were not changed.
MSG_FRAMOP_RESULT_FAILED=F-RAM {operation} did not finish: {status}.
FRAMOP_OPERATION_ERASE=erase
FRAMOP_OPERATION_SELFTEST=self-test
MSG_CONFIRM_FLASH_ERASE=The F-RAM of the kinematic brain will also be erased after the flash.
FLASHER_PREFLIGHT_FRAM_ERASE_WRONG_TIER=The F-RAM is on the kinematic brain: untick the erase for any other target.
LOG_FRAMOP_AFTER_FLASH=Erasing the F-RAM after the flash...
LBL_SECURITY_FRAMOP=Offer the erase and self-test of the F-RAM of the kinematic brain
LBL_SECURITY_FRAMOP_HELP=Off by default. Needs the SPI bridge started with --enable-fram-erase and a firmware built for it; an erase also needs the word ERASE typed in the Flasher.
MSG_SECURITY_FRAMOP_CONFIRM=Offer the F-RAM erase? An administrator will be able to write zeros over the whole F-RAM of the kinematic brain from the Flasher.
TITLE_SECURITY_FRAMOP=F-RAM of the kinematic brain
LBL_SECURITY_ESTOP_WATCH=Emergency stop watch every (ms)
LBL_SECURITY_ESTOP_WATCH_HELP=Empty or 0: off. Otherwise 500 to 60000. The server reads the physical emergency stop from the SPI bridge at that interval and shows one stop state in the apps. A change applies when the server restarts.
LBL_SECURITY_ESTOP_WATCH_INVALID=Use 0 (off) or a whole number from 500 to 60000.
FRAMOP_STATUS_DISABLED=the firmware has the F-RAM operations switched off
FRAMOP_STATUS_NO_CHIP=the firmware has no driver for the chip (no I2C timing in the build)
FRAMOP_STATUS_WRONG_SLOT=the request was not for the brain
FRAMOP_STATUS_BUSY=the brain is busy
FRAMOP_STATUS_CHIP_ERROR=the chip did not answer or did not keep what was written
FRAMOP_STATUS_VERIFY_FAIL=a cell does not read back as written
FRAMOP_STATUS_SERVER_DISABLED=the server has the F-RAM operations switched off
FRAMOP_STATUS_UNREACHABLE=the server or the bridge did not answer
FRAMOP_STATUS_UNKNOWN=unknown answer
HEADING_TLS_PIN=Certificate pinning of a server (this computer)
LBL_TLS_PIN_ENABLE=Trust only the pinned certificate of the servers listed here (off: only public certificate authorities are trusted)
LBL_TLS_PIN_ADDRESS=Server address (host or host:port)
PH_TLS_PIN_ADDRESS=For example 192.168.0.180:3000
LBL_TLS_PIN_FINGERPRINT=Fingerprint shown by the server (optional; if typed, the certificate is pinned only when it matches)
BTN_TLS_PIN=Pin the certificate
BTN_TLS_UNPIN=Remove the pin
TLS_PIN_NONE=No certificate is pinned
LBL_TLS_PIN_HELP=The certificate is read with a handshake only; nothing else is sent. With the switch on, a server with a pinned certificate is trusted only with exactly that certificate; other servers keep using the public authorities. Pins are kept in the configuration file, per server.
TLS_PIN_SAVED=Certificate settings saved
TLS_PIN_BAD_ADDRESS=Enter the server address as host or host:port
TLS_PIN_FAILED=The certificate was not pinned: {reason}
